module bindings.rust.absent_extended_header_override;

// POSIX pax extended headers exist to carry values the fixed ustar fields
// cannot, and the format gives the size record plain precedence: when an
// extended header precedes an entry, its size value is the size of that
// entry's data, overriding the octal field in the base header. A parser that
// sizes the entry's data read from the base field alone and never consults
// the extended-header records frames the same archive differently from every
// reader that honours them -- an entry can declare a small header size and a
// large extended size, and the lenient reader ends the entry early, exposing
// whatever the inflated region hides (a second header, a symlink) to its own
// entry walk.
//
// The tar crate family publishes both halves of the shape: Header::entry_size
// reads the base header's size field, and the reader's take call bounds the
// bytes handed to the entry. What is recognised here is the function that
// reads the base size and bounds a data read with it while no
// extended-header consult appears among its own call paths: in this crate
// family the consult goes through the pax_extensions iterator (a bare
// function value passed to map, a direct call, or the pax_extensions_value
// lookup helper), so the absence of that name among the call paths is
// the absence of the consult. The conditional half of the same weakness --
// the record consulted but applied only when the base field is zero -- is a
// separate binding on its own atoms and stays separate, because its
// evidence names a comparison this shape does not carry.
//
// The negation is over the function's own call paths, which the frontend
// derives from the AST: a consult in a sibling function does not clear the
// flag on the sizer, and prose in a comment satisfies neither side. It keys
// on the call-path route rather than any token mentioning the name, because
// the framing function also names the pax_extensions field when it builds the
// entry struct -- initialising that field to nothing is not consulting the
// records. Every positive clause is an exact-token match, so a call whose
// last segment merely contains the name does not qualify.
//
// Deliberately uncovered: a parser consulting the records through an
// accessor this family does not name -- the negation keys on the published
// pax_extensions surface, so a differently-named consult reads as absent.

binding absentExtendedHeaderOverride {
  query pattern presenceNode where node.scope == "function" and node.context.language == "rust" and node.context.call in ["entry_size"] and node.context.call in ["take"] and not (node.context.callPath contains "pax_extensions")
  emit issue code.AbsentExtendedHeaderOverride at node
}
